 *
 * ## Overview
 *
 * This section contains the code-level reference documentation for PicoKernel.
 * All content here is generated from Doxygen-annotated source code and kept
 * current with the codebase.
 *
 * If you have not already read the contributor documentation, the recommended
 * starting point is @ref arch_overview before reading code in any layer.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Documentation by Layer
 *
 * The table below maps each architectural layer to its API reference group.
 * Each group is generated from the corresponding source layer.
 *
 * | Layer | Group | What is documented |
 * |-------|-------|--------------------|
 * | Kernel | @subpage kernel | Allocator, scheduler, IPC, panic handler, init sequence, kernel state machine |
 * | Drivers | @subpage drivers | UART, SPI, I2C, GPIO, hardware timer, flash driver |
 * | Modules | @subpage modules | NFC, sub-GHz RF, HID, UI, IR, packet capture, Wi-Fi scan |
 * | Interfaces | @subpage interface | Serial shell, web terminal |
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Kernel
 *
 * @ref kernel "→ Kernel API Reference"
 *
 * The kernel is the architectural center of PicoKernel. All cross-layer
 * communication flows through kernel APIs. No other layer is permitted to
 * bypass it.
 *
 * | Subsystem | Description |
 * |-----------|-------------|
 * | **Allocator** | Static-pool heap allocator with canary-based corruption detection |
 * | **Scheduler** | Cooperative round-robin task scheduler |
 * | **IPC** | Inter-process communication primitives message queues |
 * | **Panic handler** | Kernel fault, boundary halts and emits diagnostic over USB |
 * | **Init** | Kernel startup sequence, hardware init, subsystem bring-up |
 * | **State machine** | Kernel operational state tracking |
 *
 * Security and memory safety requirements for kernel code: @ref security_principles.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Drivers
 *
 * @ref drivers "→ Driver API Reference"
 *
 * Drivers are thin hardware wrappers. They translate kernel requests into
 * Pico SDK calls. They contain no business logic and hold no cross-request state.
 *
 * | Driver | Hardware |
 * |--------|----------|
 * | **UART driver** | RP2350 UART peripheral serial communication |
 * | **SPI driver** | RP2350 SPI peripheral high-speed serial |
 * | **I2C driver** | RP2350 I2C peripheral low-speed device bus |
 * | **GPIO driver** | RP2350 GPIO digital input/output |
 * | **Hardware timer driver** | RP2350 timer scheduler tick source |
 * | **Flash driver** | RP2350 XIP flash backing store for LittleFS |
 * | **CYW43 driver** | CYW43 radio Wi-Fi and Bluetooth hardware |
 *
 * @note Only the kernel may call driver functions. No module, interface, or
 * other layer may call a driver directly. This is an architectural rule and
 * a review blocker. See @ref arch_overview rule 7.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Modules
 *
 * @ref modules "→ Module API Reference"
 *
 * Modules are application-level subsystems. They register tasks with the
 * kernel scheduler and communicate via IPC. They have no direct hardware access.
 *
 * | Module | Purpose | Milestone |
 * |--------|---------|-----------|
 * | **Wi-Fi scan** | Scan for visible 802.11 networks | M7 |
 * | **Packet capture** | Raw 802.11 frame capture in monitor mode | M8 |
 * | **NFC** | NFC tag read/write | Planned |
 * | **Sub-GHz RF** | Sub-GHz radio operations | Planned |
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Interfaces
 *
 * @ref interface "→ Interface API Reference"
 *
 * Interfaces are outward-facing components. They accept input from users or
 * external systems and route all requests through Kernel APIs.
 * They never call drivers directly.
 *
 * | Interface | Description | Milestone |
 * |-----------|-------------|-----------|
 * | **Serial shell** | Interactive command shell over UART | M9 |
 * | **Web terminal** | Browser-accessible terminal interface | Planned |
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Reading Order for New Code Contributors
 *
 * 1. @ref arch_overview "Architecture Overview" - understand the layer rules
 * 2. @ref coding_style "Coding Style" - understand naming and structure
 * 3. @ref docs_style "Documentation Style" - understand what is required
 * 4. @ref security_principles "Security Principles" - understand the security constraints
 * 5. Pick a layer and read its API reference
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Source Layout
 *
 * @code
 * src/
 *   kernel/         - Allocator, scheduler, IPC, panic, init
 *   drivers/        - UART, SPI, I2C, GPIO, timer, flash, CYW43
 *   modules/        - Wi-Fi, packet capture, NFC, sub-GHz
 *   interfaces/     - Serial shell, web terminal
 * include/
 *   version.h       - Single authoritative version string
 * @endcode
 *
